release_note = "First TOS release. Implemented in this version: syslog reception (UDP/TCP with RFC3164, RFC5424 and RFC6587 octet-counting framing), file collection with inode+offset state and rename/copytruncate rotation handling plus multi-line merging, durable on-disk forwarding queues with retry (at-least-once), full-text and field search, a built-in alert rule set with suppression and frequency windows, live overview counters, and the bundled VictoriaLogs engine (self-built from upstream source with a bit-reproducible recipe; no bundled runtime, no online installation). Deployment uses standard syslog ports 514 (UDP/TCP) and 6514 (TLS); the service declares exactly one Linux capability, CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE, and no other. If any port is already taken, change it in /usr/local/log-server-for-tos/log-server-for-tos.env and restart the service."
important    = "Everything runs locally; no data leaves your NAS unless you add a forwarding target. Features that depend on capabilities TOS does not expose yet (system journal access, container logs, host auditd, TOS log-center and hardware health read APIs) are shown as 'waiting for platform support' in the interface instead of being bypassed. This application does not modify the system: it does not install packages, does not change rsyslog/journald settings, does not add its service user to system groups, and does not touch the firewall. 'apt remove' keeps your data; 'apt purge' deletes the application configuration but never your data or the shared folder. Licensed MIT; the bundled VictoriaLogs is Apache-2.0 (see NOTICE)."
